# WHO MAY CUT A RELEASE. forge-ci verifies the signature on the
# `release-request/vX.Y.Z` tag against this file, in OpenSSH's allowed_signers
# format (`man ssh-keygen`, ALLOWED SIGNERS). Until it existed, both releases
# were annotated "any valid signature was accepted": the signature was checked
# for validity and against nothing else. Bead ar-1kx.18.
#
# The public key travels with the repository on purpose -- it is both the
# authorised set and the material the verification uses, so the two cannot
# drift. Adding or removing a signer is a commit, and `git blame` is the audit
# trail. That is the whole mechanism.
#
# NO `namespaces=` OPTION, deliberately: forge-ci's documented example omits it,
# and an option that narrows the namespace can only turn a good signature into
# a rejected one if the verifier names it differently. Add `valid-before=` when
# a key is retired rather than deleting the line -- old tags stay verifiable.
#
# The key is a FIDO token (`sk-`) that requires a touch, so a release cannot be
# cut by anything that merely holds a file.
